1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:03,227
I started vibecoding on February fourteenth, twenty twenty-six.

2
00:00:03,227 --> 00:00:05,282
AI helped me turn ideas into software.

3
00:00:05,282 --> 00:00:06,642
My GitHub graph turned green.

4
00:00:06,642 --> 00:00:09,591
Then I noticed a problem: the agents could keep building, but too much of

5
00:00:09,591 --> 00:00:10,952
the work still came back to me.

6
00:00:10,961 --> 00:00:11,159
I'm

7
00:00:11,159 --> 00:00:13,395
Mark Hinkle, co-founder of All Things AI.

8
00:00:13,403 --> 00:00:16,819
I've been executive director of the Node.js Foundation, VP of developer

9
00:00:16,819 --> 00:00:20,623
relations at Cloud.com, and VP of marketing at the Linux Foundation.

10
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:23,055
John Willis co-wrote The DevOps Handbook.

11
00:00:23,073 --> 00:00:26,608
He led training and services at Opscode, now Chef, worked at Enstratius,

12
00:00:26,608 --> 00:00:29,975
acquired by Dell, and co-founded SocketPlane, acquired by Docker.

13
00:00:30,261 --> 00:00:33,678
We know process, but lately we've spent more time managing than coding.

14
00:00:33,826 --> 00:00:35,991
Today, the old guys are dusting off their keyboards.

15
00:00:35,991 --> 00:00:36,170
I'm

16
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:39,515
explaining the same context, handing work between agents, checking results,

17
00:00:39,515 --> 00:00:43,627
and rescuing failed attempts. More code means more decisions waiting for me.

18
00:00:43,864 --> 00:00:46,693
The question is how to improve the system so the human doesn't become the

19
00:00:46,693 --> 00:00:48,143
bottleneck. Mini

20
00:00:48,143 --> 00:00:51,858
Gibson is our experiment. We want a small model to learn concrete handoffs,

21
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:54,776
responses to failed tests, and evidence of what changed.

22
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:58,519
We'll compare the base model, the model inside Gibson, and the tuned model

23
00:00:58,519 --> 00:01:02,570
inside Gibson. Our hypothesis is fewer requests for human help while the

24
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:04,328
work meets the same quality bar.

25
00:01:04,754 --> 00:01:05,032
Getting

26
00:01:05,032 --> 00:01:06,740
started took longer than we expected.

27
00:01:07,087 --> 00:01:09,799
We hit overloaded providers and stalled builds.

28
00:01:10,086 --> 00:01:13,422
Once we got past that, we could run our datasets and learn from actual

29
00:01:13,422 --> 00:01:18,078
failures. Our seven B model completed twenty training steps on Brev, using

30
00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:20,264
one H one hundred and NeMo Automodel.

31
00:01:20,689 --> 00:01:24,096
vLLM serves that adapter and passed connection checks.

32
00:01:24,602 --> 00:01:28,257
Separately, thirty B Lightning has a loaded adapter and development results,

33
00:01:28,493 --> 00:01:30,371
tested with Transformers and PEFT.

34
00:01:30,876 --> 00:01:32,903
We keep their datasets and results separate.

35
00:01:33,537 --> 00:01:37,539
Why The Gibson? In Hackers, it's a ridiculous supercomputer run by a bunch

36
00:01:37,539 --> 00:01:39,247
of buffoons. We thought it was apropos.

37
00:01:39,247 --> 00:01:43,317
Our Gibson defines tasks, isolates changes, runs checks, and requires

38
00:01:43,317 --> 00:01:47,041
independent review. Gibson Environment runs the tools and checks results.

39
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,658
Connecting the model is still ahead.

40
00:01:48,658 --> 00:01:52,096
The model proposes repairs; checks and approval rules stay in place.

41
00:01:52,096 --> 00:01:54,608
This is our existing Agent Kontrol dashboard.

42
00:01:55,034 --> 00:01:59,462
It shows Claude, Grok, and Codex across the fleet: working, finished, or

43
00:01:59,462 --> 00:02:02,562
waiting for me. We can see where work gets stuck.

44
00:02:03,067 --> 00:02:06,363
The experiment asks whether a specialized model can reduce the work that

45
00:02:06,363 --> 00:02:07,357
needs our attention.

46
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:11,059
For the seven B rehearsal, two hundred eighty-three source records became

47
00:02:11,059 --> 00:02:14,228
one hundred eighty-eight training rows and thirty development rows.

48
00:02:14,653 --> 00:02:18,229
Some examples were broken; others lost the answer at the token limit.

49
00:02:18,346 --> 00:02:21,058
We saved and reloaded a small LoRA adapter.

50
00:02:21,404 --> 00:02:25,167
The thirty B OpenHands track still needs its training-data record matched to

51
00:02:25,167 --> 00:02:26,659
the evaluated adapter.

52
00:02:27,163 --> 00:02:30,173
Our biggest lesson: a working connection isn't a finished job.

53
00:02:30,598 --> 00:02:34,133
The seven B adapter passed a required tool call, but failed a real

54
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:39,137
repository change. Automatic mode returned prose; forced calls repeatedly

55
00:02:39,137 --> 00:02:42,057
targeted the wrong file. No edit was applied.

56
00:02:42,244 --> 00:02:46,206
In the separate thirty B development run, the base passed six of twenty-four

57
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:48,699
tasks; the adapter passed twelve.

58
00:02:49,304 --> 00:02:51,509
Those are exploratory development results.

59
00:02:51,746 --> 00:02:55,588
We still need to match the training records, lock the comparison, and test

60
00:02:55,588 --> 00:02:59,868
on untouched tasks. Better quality and fewer human interruptions are still

61
00:02:59,868 --> 00:03:00,564
unproven.

62
00:03:00,752 --> 00:03:02,371
Three optional pieces could help.

63
00:03:02,658 --> 00:03:05,062
Nemo Gym would organize repeatable trials.

64
00:03:05,299 --> 00:03:09,093
Jev would turn locked results into advice for John and me to review.

65
00:03:09,519 --> 00:03:12,955
Langfuse would show the sequence of model calls, tools, and checks.

66
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,530
Those connections are proposed; we're not claiming they're running.

67
00:03:17,034 --> 00:03:20,629
We want useful work that passes checks and independent review, with less

68
00:03:20,629 --> 00:03:22,109
chasing and rescuing from us.

69
00:03:22,257 --> 00:03:24,800
That's how we keep the human from becoming the bottleneck.

70
00:03:25,225 --> 00:03:26,925
Follow the repository on screen.
